settlement_amount = min(net_payable, remaining_coverage_limit)
This happens in pure Python, before the LLM is ever called. The LLM's prompt explicitly says: "The settlement amount has ALREADY been calculated by the business rules. DO NOT recalculate any numbers... Use the values exactly as provided." So the LLM is only generating the natural-language sentence ("Notes") around a number it's forbidden from touching — the Notes text is genuinely LLM-written each time, not a static template, but it's constrained to paraphrase, not verify.
2. Why does Settlement Amount exactly equal "Net Payable (Replacement)" from the earlier AI Claims Analysis summary?
Because they're the same formula computed twice, not a coincidence. Both ultimately call the same helper, resolve_repair_vs_replacement():
replace_total = SUM(replacement_costs.total_replacement_estimate)
replace_net = replace_total - deductible
Whatever populated "Net Payable (Replacement)" earlier and whatever Settlement Recommendation computed both trace back to this one function. That's why they match exactly — $16,875 − $2,526 = $14,349, both places.
3. Here's the part that confirms your suspicion directly
In recommend_settlement(), there's this:
repair_replace = _get_repair_vs_replace(claim_id)   # reads repair_vs_replacement_decisions table
print("repair_replace =", repair_replace)            # ...only used for a debug log line
rvr = resolve_repair_vs_replacement(cur_rvr, claim_id)  # ← this is what's ACTUALLY used
is_replacement = rvr["is_replacement"]
Settlement Recommendation fetches the Repair-vs-Replacement agent's actual stored decision — and then never uses it for anything except a debug print. It re-derives its own "is this Repair or Replace" verdict completely independently, straight from the raw loss_assessments/replacement_costs tables, using its own formula:
