In your test, both happened to agree (Replace), so it looked like clean data hand-off. But if an adjuster had manually overridden the decision in repair_vs_replacement_decisions.decision (e.g. forced "Repair" for a business reason), Settlement Recommendation would silently ignore that override and use its own recalculation instead. That's a real data-passing gap between these two agents.
4. Your Financial Leakage doubt — you remembered correctly
You're right that Financial Leakage isn't comparing against a genuinely independent "actual" cost. For Replace-decision claims pre-dispatch, its "actual cost" is the exact same replacement_costs sum that resolve_repair_vs_replacement() and the Repair-vs-Replace agent itself both already used. There's no real ground-truth cross-check happening — it's the same AI-estimated number being compared against itself. The only genuinely independent value anywhere in this pipeline is a real vendor invoice (expert_estimates), which only exists after dispatch.
5. Repair vs Replacement page — the actual logic
Per damaged item, one single LLM call generates both repair_cost and replacement_cost together:
prompt = f"""Estimate realistic repair and replacement costs ...
Item Type: {item_type}, Item Age: {item_age}, Severity: {severity}, 
Adjuster's Notes: {adjuster_notes}, Initial Damage Estimate: ${estimated_cost}"""
There's no real pricing database behind this — it's an unconstrained LLM guess per item, which matches what you remembered ("the AI agent was giving a lot of different answers"). These per-item numbers get summed into totals.
The final Repair vs Replace recommendation, though, is a deterministic rule on top of those LLM totals — not an LLM judgment:
if total_repair_cost < (total_replacement_cost * 0.40): "Repair"
elif total_replacement_cost <= (total_repair_cost * 1.25): "Replace"
elif useful_life_remaining < 3: "Replace"
else: "Replace"   # ← note: the fallback is ALSO "Replace"
Worth noticing: structurally, this can basically never recommend "Repair" unless repair cost comes in under 40% of replacement cost — every other branch, including the final else, resolves to "Replace."
And "Confidence: 85%" is not an AI confidence measure at all — it's pure arithmetic:
confidence_score = min(1.0, 0.5 + |repair_cost - replacement_cost| / replacement_cost)
It literally just measures how far apart the two cost guesses are. A bigger gap between repair and replacement costs produces a higher "confidence" score. It says nothing about whether either estimate is realistic.
